{"parameters":{"model":"dxn:com.anthropic.model.claude-opus-5.default","stream":true,"tools":[{"name":"eval","description":"Runs code against the workspace and returns whatever that code printed. If the code throws, the call fails with what it printed before the throw, followed by the error. This is the only tool: every read, every write and every operation happens inside it. Printed output is capped (at 8000 characters by default) and cut off beyond that, so print the values you need rather than whole objects or long lists.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"code":{"type":"string","description":"The program, in the dialect the system prompt describes. Print anything you need to see."}},"required":["code"],"additionalProperties":true}}]},"prompt":{"content":[{"options":{},"role":"system","content":"## Code mode\n\nYou have exactly one tool, `eval`, which runs the body of an async JavaScript function against\nthis workspace and returns whatever that code printed. Nothing else is visible to you: a value\nyou do not `print` never reaches you. Do not use `import` or `require` — the API below is\nin scope.\n\nPrefer one `eval` call that does the whole job — query, inspect, change, print — over a call\nper step. Print the specific values you need to reason about, not whole objects, and keep the\noutput small. Stay on the objects the request names or the conversation is attached to: reading\nor changing others the request did not ask about spends calls and risks unasked-for writes.\n\n### API\n\n- `print(...values)` — adds a line to this call's output. Strings go through verbatim. An\n  object prints as a card: its type, id, label and fields, refs as `→ type id \"label\"`. Print\n  the object itself, never `JSON.stringify` it. Anything else prints as JSON, long strings and\n  arrays cut with a `…[+N chars]` / `… N more` marker.\n- `print(await describe(obj))` — the card plus counts of the object's relations, children and\n  referrers, with the call that fetches each — the only way to see that those exist.\n- `await relations(obj, 'out' | 'in', typename?)`, `await children(obj)`,\n  `await referrers(obj, typename?, field?)` — the relations from or to an object, the objects\n  it is the parent of, and the objects whose ref fields point at it.\n- `await query(typename, props?)` — objects of that typename, optionally narrowed by exact\n  property values, e.g. `await query('example.com/type/Task', { status: 'open' })`.\n- `await make(typename, props)` — a new object; it is not stored until you add it.\n- `await add(obj)` / `await remove(obj)` — store or delete an object.\n- `update(obj, (obj) => { obj.field = value; })` — the only way to change a stored object.\n- `await flush()` — waits for pending writes to land; call it before printing a final\n  confirmation.\n- `ref(obj)` — a reference to an object you hold. A `Ref<typename>` field takes one, never\n  an id or a URI string: `await make('example.com/type/Task', { title: 'Review', owner: ref(person) })`.\n  Where an operation's input takes references, pass `ref(obj)` too.\n\n### Types\n\nTypes are not listed here: find them, and their fields, with the Discovery skill's `queryTypes`.\n\n### Operations\n\nThe skills above describe their capabilities as tools; in code mode they are NOT tools. Each one\nis an async function on `ops`, taking one argument matching its parameter schema. A failed\noperation throws, so wrap a call you expect to fail in `try`/`catch`. The names are kebab-case,\nso they are indexed rather than dotted.\n\n- `await ops[\"discovery-load-skill\"](input)` — Without a key, lists the skills you can use. With one, returns that skill's instructions and operations, enabling it in this conversation if it is not yet.\n  input: {\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"key\":{\"description\":\"The skill to load, as `loadSkill()` lists it.\",\"type\":\"string\"}},\"additionalProperties\":false}\n- `await ops[\"discovery-query-operations\"](input)` — Searches the operations of the skills you can use. With `keys`, returns each one in full: its input and the code that calls it.\n  input: {\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"query\":{\"description\":\"Words describing what the operation does.\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"skill\":{\"description\":\"Only this skill's operations.\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"keys\":{\"description\":\"Operations to return in full.\",\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}},\"additionalProperties\":false}\n- `await ops[\"discovery-query-types\"](input)` — Searches the registered types. With `typenames`, returns their fields.\n  input: {\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"query\":{\"description\":\"Words in the type or field names.\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"typenames\":{\"description\":\"Types to return with their fields, by typename or DXN.\",\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}},\"additionalProperties\":false}\n\n## Skills Definitions\n\n<skill>\n  Nothing about this workspace is listed in this prompt. Its skills, their operations and its types are found with\n  the three operations below, which code calls like any other.\n  \n  - `loadSkill()` lists the skills you can use: those enabled in this conversation, and those you may enable. An\n    enabled skill carries the rules for its part of the work, so at the start of a request load each one the request\n    touches with `loadSkill({ key })`, and follow its instructions. Loading a skill that is not enabled enables it;\n    its operations can be called from your next eval call.\n  - `queryOperations({ query })` searches those skills' operations by what they do; `queryOperations({ skill })`\n    lists one skill's. A row says whether you can call it now. Look an operation up with\n    `queryOperations({ keys: [key] })` before calling it: that gives its input and the code that calls it.\n  - `queryTypes({ query })` searches the registered types; `queryTypes({ typenames: [typename] })` gives their\n    fields.\n  \n  Find what a request needs in one eval call that prints it, then do the work in the next.\n</skill>"},{"options":{},"role":"user","content":"Every task of type com.example.type.task whose title mentions docs must be closed: set its status to \"done\". Then tell me how many tasks are done in total."},{"options":{},"role":"assistant","content":[{"options":{},"type":"text","text":"I'll start by exploring the workspace to understand the task type and find relevant tasks."},{"options":{},"type":"tool-call","id":"toolu_01KH3LwXfEnrnFY4cMfayxhb","name":"eval","params":{"code":"\nconst types = await ops[\"discovery-query-types\"]({ typenames: [\"com.example.type.task\"] });\nprint(JSON.stringify(types, null, 2));\n"},"providerExecuted":false},{"options":{},"type":"tool-call","id":"toolu_0121hvx4tN14Zg5hvT3W5JQj","name":"eval","params":{"code":"\nconst skills = await ops[\"discovery-load-skill\"]({});\nprint(JSON.stringify(skills, null, 2));\n"},"providerExecuted":false}]},{"options":{},"role":"tool","content":[{"options":{},"type":"tool-result","id":"toolu_01KH3LwXfEnrnFY4cMfayxhb","name":"eval","isFailure":false,"result":"{\n  \"types\": [\n    {\n      \"typename\": \"com.example.type.task\",\n      \"dxn\": \"dxn:com.example.type.task:0.1.0\",\n      \"kind\": \"object\",\n      \"fields\": \"title: string, status: string, priority?: number\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","providerExecuted":false},{"options":{},"type":"tool-result","id":"toolu_0121hvx4tN14Zg5hvT3W5JQj","name":"eval","isFailure":false,"result":"{\n  \"skills\": []\n}","providerExecuted":false}]},{"options":{},"role":"assistant","content":[{"options":{},"type":"reasoning","text":"No skills to use here, so I'll go straight to querying the tasks.\n\n"},{"options":{},"type":"text","text":"The type has `title`, `status`, `priority`. Let me look at all tasks before changing anything."},{"options":{},"type":"tool-call","id":"toolu_01MvMJdenFmbBkpZZ3u4qNss","name":"eval","params":{"code":"\nconst tasks = await query('com.example.type.task');\npri